# Interview Follow-ups

## Q1. Can we solve this without hashing?

Yes.

Nested loops.

Time

```text
O(J × S)
```

---

## Q2. Why is HashSet optimal?

Because:

- Average lookup is **O(1)**.
- Jewels are stored only once.
- No unnecessary values are maintained.

---

## Q3. What if jewel lookup had to support counts?

Then use

```java
HashMap<Character, Integer>
```

instead of a set.

---

## Q4. What if the input contains only ASCII characters?

An alternative solution is a boolean lookup table.

```java
class Solution {

    public int numJewelsInStones(String jewels, String stones) {

        boolean[] isJewel = new boolean[128];

        for (char ch : jewels.toCharArray()) {
            isJewel[ch] = true;
        }

        int count = 0;

        for (char ch : stones.toCharArray()) {
            if (isJewel[ch]) {
                count++;
            }
        }

        return count;
    }
}
```

This avoids hashing overhead and is often the fastest implementation for ASCII input.

